ping: without libcap, a ping with no privilege makes no id calls

Carried by Kryptik against iputils 20250605; not upstream at the time of
writing. Built without libcap, ping lowers its effective uid with seteuid()
at start, raises and lowers it around socket creation, and ends with
setuid(getuid()), for a setuid-root install. A ping that is neither setuid
nor given file capabilities starts with its effective uid equal to its real
one and has nothing to lower, yet each call still reaches the kernel, and
the first or last one failing ends the program. A sandbox that refuses id
calls therefore stops ping before its first packet, although the ICMP
datagram socket it uses there needs no privilege.

When ping starts with equal effective and real uids, the three calls are
skipped. A setuid install behaves as before.

--- a/ping/ping_common.c
+++ b/ping/ping_common.c
@@ -134,7 +134,7 @@
 #endif
 	rts->uid = getuid();
 #ifndef HAVE_LIBCAP
-	if (seteuid(rts->uid))
+	if (euid != rts->uid && seteuid(rts->uid))
 		error(-1, errno, "setuid");
 #endif
 }
@@ -177,6 +177,8 @@
 #else
 int modify_capability(int on)
 {
+	if (euid == getuid())
+		return 0;
 	if (seteuid(on ? euid : getuid())) {
 		error(0, errno, "seteuid");
 		return -1;
@@ -194,7 +196,7 @@
 		error(-1, errno, "cap_set_proc");
 	cap_free(cap);
 #else
-	if (setuid(getuid()))
+	if (euid != getuid() && setuid(getuid()))
 		error(-1, errno, "setuid");
 #endif
 }
